  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery revealed stratus had dissipated as of\nnoon, with surface observations showing south winds continuing\nto increase as expected. Lee cyclogenesis in northeast Colorado\nwill continue to support strong south winds through sunset,\ngusting easily in the 40-50 mph range - especially with full\ninsolation avaiable for mixing now. Afternoon temperatures will\nonce again easily warm well above normal, well into the 60s. \n\nOne more unseasonably warm November night tonight, as south\nwinds remain elevated with a low level jet and boundary layer\nmixing deterring radiational cooling. Sunrise Saturday\ntemperatures will again struggle to get below 50 degrees - near\nrecord warm minimums for late November. \n\nSurface low pressure in the vicinity of SW KS will produce much\nweakened winds Saturday morning. Incoming strong shortwave\ntrough near Grand Canyon, Arizona 6 am Saturday, will be near \nthe Four Corners at noon, and enter SW KS 6 pm Saturday. This is\na little slower than previous solutions, and will help support \nshower and thunderstorm coverage focusing on the southeast half \nof SW KS Saturday afternoon/night. 12z RRFS forecasts marginal \ninstability, typical of November, with dewpoints of 50-55 and\nMU CAPE near 500 J/kg - from Ford County southeastward, after \n3 pm Saturday. Bulk shear and kinematics are of course strong \nahead of the incoming shortwave, and will easily support \norganized convection, should instability support updrafts. \n12z RRFS suggests the strong forcing for ascent will intercept \nwhat instability is available, about 6 pm, after which time NBM \npops increase sharply across the southeast half of the CWA. Some\nlinear line segments are probable, supporting the marginal 5% \nwind probability from SPC (Dodge City southeastward), with \nmarginally severe hail being a lesser concern. Model consensus \nsuggests the cold front will clear the southern border by \nmidnight Saturday night. Elevated north winds and much colder \ntemperatures are expected sunrise Sunday, with wind chills in \nthe 20s. \n\nMuch colder Sunday with diminishing north winds as cold surface\nhigh pressure builds south into Kansas. 12z GFS cools 850 mb\ntemperatures to near 0C midday Sunday, so even with full\nsunshine, temperatures will struggle into the 40s Sunday\nafternoon. 1030 mb surface ridging will provide near ideal\nradiational cooling Monday morning, with finally the first\nwidespread killing freeze of the fall season, well down into \nthe 20s. \n\nNext shortwave over Nevada 6 am Monday is modelled to track\nquickly southeast, reaching SW KS about 6 am Tuesday. Some\nmodels strengthen this shortwave some as it arrives in Kansas\nTuesday, others keep it more open and weaker. But model\nconsensus continues to support the notion of a progressive, \nweak and moisture-starved system. NBM pops for light snow are \nnecessarily quite limited, and impacts/snow amounts are \nexpected to be minimal to none at this time. The primary impact \nwill be the fact that the atmosphere will be cold enough to \nsupport snow, with NBM/MEX keeping most locations in the 30s all\nday Tuesday. Please continue to follow trusted sources for \nweather information regarding this system during peak \nThanksgiving travel next week.",
